  "questionId": "q13",
  "speechText": "For me, I try to control latency and cost in several layers. The main reason is that every extra model call, extra token, or unnecessary retry can make the system slower and more expensive. For example, I use normal backend logic, filtering, and retrieval instead of asking the model to do everything. In Clincove, I also worked with prompt caching so we could reuse stable context instead of processing the same context again. Because of that, I also try to keep the context small and retrieve only the relevant and authorized data instead of sending everything to the model. At the same time, I use timeouts and bounded retries. I only retry temporary errors like timeouts, rate limits, or provider overload, and I avoid retrying permanent errors. For longer workflows, I also use checkpoint and recovery, so if one part fails, we can continue from the completed part instead of running the whole job again. So overall, I track the model used, token usage, retry attempts, latency, and estimated cost, and use that real data to decide what to optimize next. Flow nhớ nhanh: reduce AI work → cache → smaller context → timeout retry → checkpoint → measure Điểm mạnh nhất của bản này là gần như mọi ý chính đều có hands-on evidence thật của anh, chứ không chỉ là best practice chung.",
